{"name":"Tropical Fog","hex":"#cbcab6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tropical","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the tropics, the equatorial region between 23 degrees north and 23 degrees south.","From, or similar to, a hot, humid climate."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tropical plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tropical"},{"term":"fog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick cloud that forms near the ground; the obscurity of such a cloud.","A mist or film clouding a surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with or as if with fog.","To become obscured in condensation or water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tropics","blocks":[{"text":"The tropics are the region of Earth surrounding the equator, where the Sun may shine directly overhead. This contrasts with the temperate or polar regions of Earth, where the Sun can never be directly overhead. Because of Earth's axial tilt, the width of the tropics is twice the tilt. The tropics are also referred to as the tropical zone and the torrid zone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png/330px-World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropics","wikidata":"Q42530"},{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog","wikidata":"Q37477"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Bora_Bora.jpg/500px-Bora_Bora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Bora_Bora.jpg/500px-Bora_Bora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aerial view of Bora Bora in French Polynesia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABora_Bora.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg/500px-Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg/1280px-Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg/500px-Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tropical sunset over the sea in Kota Kinabalu in Malaysia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png/500px-Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png/1280px-Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png/500px-Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Graph showing the zonally averaged monthly precipitation. The tropics receive more precipitation than higher latitudes. The precipitation maximum, which follows the solar equator through the year, is under the rising branch of the Hadley circulation. The sub-tropical minima are under the descending branch and cause the formation of desert areas.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMonthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Paju%C3%A7ara.jpg/250px-Paju%C3%A7ara.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Paju%C3%A7ara.jpg/500px-Paju%C3%A7ara.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Paju%C3%A7ara.jpg/250px-Paju%C3%A7ara.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APaju%C3%A7ara.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg/250px-Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg/500px-Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg/250px-Pitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APitaya_cross_section_ed2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thum